### **The Glitch Isn’t the System. It’s the Contract You Quietly Signed.**

We’re the first generation to grow up with super-intelligence in our pockets and still sit in lecture halls optimised for a world that no longer exists.

That mismatch isn’t subtle. It’s structural.

Universities are still grading obedience, memorisation, and speed — skills that made sense when information was scarce, and authority was centralised. Meanwhile, the economy outside the campus gates rewards synthesis, judgment, and the ability to learn faster than the environment changes.

Here’s the uncomfortable part:\
*The system isn’t going to update itself in time for you.*

And that means responsibility lands somewhere awkward.

> *As neuroscientist Lisa Feldman Barrett puts it:*\
*“Sometimes we’re responsible for things not because they’re our fault, but because we’re the only ones who can change them.”*

The outdated OS isn’t your fault.\
Running it without modification is your choice.

If you treat university like a checklist — attend class, memorise slides, pass exams — you may graduate on time and still arrive late to the future. A high GPA is no longer the finish line. It’s the baseline.

Differentiation is the real degree.

### **Your Mind Is Software. Most Students Never Update It.**

You can’t run 2025 applications on 1990 hardware and blame the app when it crashes.

Yet that’s exactly what most students do.

They optimise for grades instead of capability. They mistake familiarity for mastery. They confuse motion with progress. Over time, they accumulate something dangerous:&nbsp;**learning debt**&nbsp;— knowledge that works in exams but collapses under real pressure.

If you want leverage in the AI era, you have to treat learning like software:\
audit what you know, patch what’s broken, and upgrade continuously — even when the syllabus says you’re “done.”

Here’s the framework I use to do exactly that.

### **The Four Stages of Competence (And Where Most People Stall)**

Most students study to pass.\
You need to study to evolve.

The Four Stages of Competence turn learning from a vague obligation into a navigable system — but only if you’re honest about where you are.

**1. Unconsciously Unskilled — The Unknown Unknowns**

This is where you don’t know what you don’t know.

The trap here is comfort. Sticking strictly to the syllabus feels safe, but syllabi are often years behind reality. They reflect what was stable, not what is emerging.

The hack is exploring the&nbsp;**adjacent possible**&nbsp;— the edges of your discipline where new value forms. Computer science collides with biology. Law collides with code. Psychology collides with product design.

That overlap is where futures are hiding.

Use AI here not as a tutor, but as a radar:\
**“What are three disruptive ideas outside my major that will likely reshape my career within five years?”**

If nothing it says scares you a little, you’re not looking far enough.

**2. Consciously Unskilled — The Gap**

This is the moment of friction — when you realise you don’t understand something that matters.

It feels like incompetence. Most people interpret it as a signal to stop.

That’s the mistake.

Avoidance doesn’t protect you. Avoidance is stagnation. Eliminating all potential threats also means eliminating all potential for growth.

This stage isn’t failure; it’s information. Discomfort is the download bar telling you exactly what you need next.

Here’s where most students ask the wrong question.\
They ask&nbsp;*why*&nbsp;they’re struggling.

Research on self-awareness shows why-questions often trap us:

- they pull us toward limitations,
- stir negative emotion,
- anchor us in the past.

Instead, ask&nbsp;*what*:

- **What specifically don’t I understand?**
- **What assumption is breaking down?**
- **What skill would make this easier next time?**

Why helps explain the world.\
What helps you change yourself.

Use AI accordingly:\
**“Explain this concept like I’m 12, then 18, then an expert — and show me where people usually get it wrong.”**

**3. Consciously Skilled — The Grind**

Now you understand — but only with effort.

This is where shortcuts are most tempting and most destructive.

You cannot automate understanding. You cannot outsource intuition. You cannot copy-paste your way to judgment.

AI misuse here doesn’t just slow growth; it actively makes you worse by eroding the mental muscle that expertise requires.

The rule is simple:\
AI can challenge your thinking, but it cannot replace it.

Use it as a sparring partner, not a ghostwriter:\
**“Here is my reasoning. Tear it apart. Where is it weak? What am I overconfident about?”**

If you’re not willing to be wrong in private, you’ll be exposed in public later.

**4. Unconsciously Skilled — The Upgrade**

Eventually, the skill becomes automatic.

This is where grades improve — and growth quietly dies.

An ‘A’ is not mastery. It’s just proof you solved a local problem under fixed conditions. Comfort here is seductive, but it’s also a ceiling.

The move isn’t to abandon depth for novelty. It’s to automate what should be automatic so your attention can attack harder problems — problems without answer keys.

Some skills deserve obsession.\
Others should be background processes.

Knowing the difference is a competitive advantage.

### **When You Fail (And You Will), Debug Instead of Spiralling**

The system is buggy. You will bomb a test, misjudge a project, or realise too late that your strategy was wrong.

When that happens, don’t brute-force harder studying. That’s just panic disguised as effort.

Use the APR protocol:

A**wareness:**&nbsp;Identify the failure honestly. Was it effort, strategy, or understanding?

P**ause:**&nbsp;Resist the reflex to double down blindly. Friction demands analysis.

R**eframe:**&nbsp;Failure isn’t a personality flaw. It’s user feedback.

Your job isn’t to feel better.\
It’s to patch the system.

### **The Real Point of a Degree**

A degree might get you to the interview.\
It won’t make you irreplaceable.

That comes from intent — from treating learning as an active experiment rather than a passive requirement.

Don’t just be a student of your discipline. Be a researcher of your own potential. Use modern tools to compress learning cycles, test assumptions quickly, and explore questions the syllabus never asks.

The future doesn’t reward those who follow instructions perfectly.

It rewards those who noticed the OS was outdated — and took responsibility for upgrading anyway.

### **Further Reading & References:**

- Broadwell, M. M. (1969).&nbsp;*Teaching for Learning (XVI)*.
